<dependencies>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-thymeleaf</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Open HTML to PDF -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>com.openhtmltopdf</groupId>
        <artifactId>openhtmltopdf-core</artifactId>
        <version>1.0.10</version>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>com.openhtmltopdf</groupId>
        <artifactId>openhtmltopdf-pdfbox</artifactId>
        <version>1.0.10</version>
    </dependency>
    <!-- ICU4J-backed bidi splitting/reordering: correct text-run layout and alignment -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>com.openhtmltopdf</groupId>
        <artifactId>openhtmltopdf-rtl-support</artifactId>
        <version>1.0.10</version>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>

openhtmltopdf-core — the brain. Parses your XHTML + CSS and computes the layout: page breaks, margins, repeating table headers. Knows nothing about PDF itself.
openhtmltopdf-pdfbox — the hand. Takes core's computed layout and draws it into an actual PDF using Apache PDFBox. Also does the font embedding. Without it you have a layout and no output format.
openhtmltopdf-rtl-support — the linguist. Brings ICU4J bidi so mixed-direction text (Arabic names, Hebrew, RTL punctuation) is split and ordered correctly. If your statements are 100% Latin/LTR, you could drop it — but for banking, customer names make it worth keeping.
Production? Yes, all three are runtime dependencies, needed in the deployed jar, not just for tests.
